{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgauGtzKc28XC6JbmZnJi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Short answer:** I know of no result for $V_f(x,y)$ itself, meaning the sum over moduli $q=f(k)$ weighted by $f'(k)$. I cannot name a paper, thesis, or preprint that states a main term, constant, or error term for it. Everything below is related work, recalled from memory with no way to check it. I give no precise exponents where I don't trust them.\n\n## 1. Squarefree numbers in progressions: single modulus and averages\n\nWrite $E(x;q,l)=\\#\\{n\\le x \\text{ squarefree},\\, n\\equiv l \\ (q)\\} - \\text{main term}$. The main term depends on $\\gcd(l,q)$. For $\\gcd(l,q)=1$ it is $\\frac{x}{q}\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\zeta(2)}\\prod_{p\\mid q}(1-p^{-2})^{-1}$.\n\n- **Pointwise bounds.** The Möbius/hyperbola argument gives $E\\ll\\sqrt{x}$ trivially. Prachar and later authors improved this in various ranges of $q$. Confidence that these results exist: moderate. Confidence in the exact statements: low.\n- **Hooley (Bull. London Math. Soc., c. 1975, \"A note on square-free numbers in arithmetic progressions\").** He gives a nontrivial bound for the second moment $\\sum_{l \\bmod q}E(x;q,l)^2$ that beats the trivial $q\\,x$ bound for suitable $q$. Confidence in the attribution: fairly good. Confidence in the exponents: low, so I won't state them.\n- **Blomer (Quart. J. Math., c. 2008, on divisor sums in arithmetic progressions).** He proves bounds for the second moment averaged over $q\\le Q$, and the squarefree case is a consequence or an analogue. Confidence: moderate.\n- **Nunes (Ramon Nunes, c. 2015–16, squarefree numbers in arithmetic progressions).** He gives improved bounds for the second moment, and I believe also for the variance over $q$ in some ranges. Confidence in the attribution: moderate. Confidence in the content: low.\n- **Le Boudec (Pierre Le Boudec, c. 2014–15).** I recall work on the variance of squarefree numbers in progressions over moduli $q\\le Q$, possibly with an asymptotic in a limited range of $Q$ relative to $x$. Confidence: low. I do not trust any constant or range I might state.\n\n## 2. All moduli, and the prime analogue\n\n- **Primes, all moduli (Barban–Linnik–Tshudakov / Montgomery–Hooley).** For $x/(\\log x)^A\\le Q\\le x$,\n  $$\\sum_{q\\le Q}\\sum^*_{a \\bmod q}\\Big(\\pi(x;q,a)-\\tfrac{\\mathrm{li}(x)}{\\varphi(q)}\\Big)^2\\sim Qx\\log Q.$$\n  Confidence: high for the shape, since this is the Montgomery–Hooley asymptotic. Refinements with lower-order terms are due to Friedlander–Goldston (c. 1996), and Fouvry and others also worked on this. Confidence in the details: moderate.\n- **Squarefree, all moduli $q\\le Q$.** I believe the expected behaviour is a main term of size roughly $c\\,x^{1/2}Q^{3/2}$ in some range. That is only a heuristic from memory. I am **not confident** of the exponent or the constant, and I can't cite the theorem that proves it.\n- **Primes, sparse moduli (primes $q$, or $q=f(k)$).** I know of no sharp asymptotic variance result for sparse moduli such as polynomial values. Variances over prime moduli exist in the large-sieve and Bombieri–Vinogradov-type literature, but I won't attribute a specific statement.\n\n## 3. Summary of confidence\n\n| Item | Confidence |\n|---|---|\n| No known result for $V_f$ (to me) | High that I know none. I cannot rule out a recent preprint. |\n| Montgomery–Hooley asymptotic for primes | High |\n| Hooley, Blomer, Nunes, Le Boudec exist and treat squarefree variance | Moderate to low |\n| Exact exponents, ranges, constants for squarefree results | Low, so omitted |\n\nTo settle this, search for Nunes, Le Boudec, Blomer, and Hooley on arXiv/MathSciNet, plus citations of Hooley's 1975 note. I can't do that from here."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3714,"cache_read_input_tokens":649,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3714},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b608de2-9fd2-4684-a5ef-f6c72f2c8596","uuid":"84540b91-e109-4863-8611-b92779b8c2ee","timestamp":"2026-10-31T19:55:32.189Z","request_id":"req_011CgauGtr8T5W4zBWw5yJBn"}
